WITNESSED SUBJECT SOURCE:

As climate and conflict collide, Indigenous leaders confront overlapping crises at the UN
URL: https://ictnews.org/news/as-climate-and-conflict-collide-indigenous-leaders-confront-overlapping-crises-at-the-un/
This story was originally published by Grist. Te Aniwaniwa Paterson & Dionne Phillips Grist This story is published through the Indigenous News Alliance. Although itâ€™s been nearly 20 years since more than 100 countries adopted the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples, the rights and lives of those it is meant to protect remain under constant threat from compounding crises. Indigenous lands are battered by&nbsp; record-breaking storms , dessicated by drought, and vanishing under rising seas. Amid these climate-driven threats, Indigenous peoples continue dying by the hundreds in wars and&nbsp; while defending their lands , even as they are&nbsp; persecuted by government officials .&nbsp; â€œClimate change, militarization, extractivism, and legal marginalization reinforce one another,â€ said Binota Moy Dhamai, who is Tripura from the Chittagong Hill Tracts in Bangladesh, and a former chair of the United Nations Expert Mechanism on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples, or&nbsp; EMRIP . This week, hundreds of Indigenous delegates gather in Geneva at the 19th annual gathering of that United Nations body to discuss implementing solutions to these abuses and push all countries to more proactively protect Indigenous peoples everywhere. EMRIP, one of three U.N. organizations focused exclusively on Indigenous peoples, is composed of seven Indigenous experts, each representing a different region. As part of the U.N. Human Rights Council, they focus on developing the data, recommendations, and advice to shape international standards regarding the fundamental rights of Indigenous peoples and support their enforcement.&nbsp; This yearâ€™s&nbsp; session &nbsp;will see Indigenous leaders, governments, and experts come together for a week of discussions and debates. The weekâ€™s meetings will cover both longstanding and emerging issues, including inadequate disaster relief, artificial intelligence, and resource extraction. For many Indigenous leaders around the world, the stakes could not be any higher, given the overlapping existential threats of violent conflict and climate change.&nbsp;&nbsp; â€œIndigenous peoples always face the brunt of conflict,â€ said Valmaine Toki, who is MÄori and the EMRIP member representing the Pacific region. Conflict, she said, leads to the dispossession of, and alienation from, their traditional lands even as the people from whom they are stolen are drawn into those fights. â€œItâ€™s a lose-lose situation for Indigenous peoples on all fronts.â€ This year, those meeting in Geneva face a growing list of intersecting issues, including militarization, climate change, exploitation by extractive industries, biodiversity decline, shrinking civic space, and digital transformation, Dhamai said.&nbsp; For Indigenous peoples in the Pacific, for instance, climate change and colonization are inextricably linked. Those from the South Pacific nation of New Caledonia have been part of the Pacific-led effort to hold large countries responsible for the climate crisis and its impact. Viro Xulue is Kanak and the Human Rights and Indigenous Rights advisor for the Drehu Customary Council, which is a traditional Kanak governance body. He said such work is part of the ongoing fight for true independence. â€œItâ€™s important for us in the process of decolonization because itâ€™s to respect the self-determination and what we want for our future, what we want for our children, and what we want to protect our resources, our moana, and our dream for the next generation,â€ Xulue said. Despite legal victories like an International Court of Justice&nbsp; advisory opinion &nbsp;that said the countries contributing to climate change are accountable for the damage it has caused, the Indigenous people of New Caledonia continue fighting for their inherent rights. â€œThe inequity for Kanak people is always here since 170 years of colonization,â€ Xulue said. â€œThe injustice is always here for the Indigenous Kanak. They didnâ€™t resolve the problem of the colonization and the impact of the colonization.â€&nbsp; Although the challenges facing Indigenous peoples vary across regions, those attending EMRIP plan to discuss several common themes. One of them is the ongoing fight for Indigenous involvement in government decision-making. Many countries do not follow international standards, and there is widespread agreement among Indigenous peoples that say they should be consulted and included in discussions that could impact them and their land.&nbsp; In northern Ontario, for example, the Canadian government has granted mining rights on the vast wetland ecosystem known as the Breathing Lands. It is part of the Ring of Fire mineral deposit and holds enormous stores of nickel and other metals. It is also a massive carbon sink and a key biodiversity hub. Extraction projects are proceeding despite strong resistance from First Nations in the area, including the Nishnawbe Aski Nation, that represents 49 communities. Kohen Mattinas of Lac Seul First Nation and Constance Lake First Nation is representing Nishnawbe Aski Nation at EMRIP and says that the right of all Indigenous peoples to free, prior, and informed consent must be respected. Mattinas is also the co-founder of Okiniwak, a youth organization created in 2025 in response to Canadian legislation that allows projects to be â€œadvanced through an accelerated process.â€ In addition to its international advocacy, Okiniwak organizes rallies in Indigenous communities and at legislative buildings. â€œWeâ€™ve taken it upon ourselves to protect the land and our rights on the front lines,â€ he said. For Mattinas, attending EMRIP is an important part of that work. â€œThrough speaking at these international forums, weâ€™re also able to connect with other Indigenous people around the world who are either facing the same things, or they face similar things, and we just kind of learn from each other, bring back that knowledge home, or we are able to collaborate in ways to strengthen both of our messaging or our work,â€ he said.&nbsp; Those connections can be even more valuable for Indigenous people from countries that do not recognize their legal status, like Botswana. Nichodimas Cooper, who is Indigenous Nama from that nation, said that lack of recognition makes it difficult to bring change on the national level. Cooper said his people face forced assimilation and displacement, and EMRIP offers a unique opportunity to pressure countries to respect their inalienable rights. â€œWe find it as a very important platform that empowers Indigenous peoples like us,â€ he said. â€œWe hardly have such a privilege as Indigenous communities to resource ourselves to attend these platforms and be able to inform ourselves on the mechanisms or platforms that are able to make impact within our advocacyâ€™s hope.â€ But as Indigenous delegates continue building solidarity, new threats, like artificial intelligence, are emerging. That has led to a push for data sovereignty and stronger protections against technologies being developed without free, prior, and informed consent.&nbsp; Dhamai said he hopes EMRIP will provide guidance on these mounting issues, alongside&nbsp; climate finance &nbsp;and the global energy transition, because Indigenous perspectives must shape international standards rather than respond to them.&nbsp; This yearâ€™s session is also expected to be shaped by reflections on the approaching&nbsp; 20th anniversary &nbsp;of UNDRIP next year. Nearly two decades after the declarationâ€™s adoption, the central question facing delegates remains remarkably similar to the one asked in 2007: not whether Indigenous rights should be recognized, but whether governments are prepared to uphold them. The post As climate and conflict collide, Indigenous leaders confront overlapping crises at the UN appeared first on ICT .

CURRENT BODY:

Hundreds of Indigenous delegates have gathered in Geneva for the 19th annual session of the United Nations Expert Mechanism on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples (EMRIP) to address the converging threats of climate breakdown, militarization, and resource extraction. [As climate and conflict collide, Indigenous leaders confront overlapping crises at the UN](https://ictnews.org/news/as-climate-and-conflict-collide-indigenous-leaders-confront-overlapping-crises-at-the-un/). This meeting occurs nearly two decades after the adoption of the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples, yet participants report that the rights the declaration promises remain under siege. The discussions reveal a pattern where environmental degradation and violent conflict do not merely coexist but actively reinforce one another, disproportionately impacting communities in the Global South who are least responsible for the crisis.

## The Compounding Nature of Crisis

The central argument emerging from the Geneva session is that climate change, militarization, extractivism, and legal marginalization function as a single, compounding system rather than isolated challenges. Binota Moy Dhamai, a former chair of EMRIP from the Chittagong Hill Tracts in Bangladesh, noted that these forces reinforce one another, creating a trap for Indigenous peoples. Valmaine Toki, the EMRIP member representing the Pacific region, described this dynamic as a "lose-lose situation" where conflict leads to the dispossession of traditional lands even as the people are drawn into the fighting. The source text highlights that Indigenous lands are currently being battered by record-breaking storms, dried out by drought, and submerged by rising seas, all while hundreds of Indigenous people die in wars or while defending their territories against persecution by government officials. This convergence means that a community facing a climate-driven disaster often finds its capacity to respond crippled by simultaneous military conflict or legal exclusion.

## Extraction Over Consent

A primary fault line exposed during the proceedings is the continued advancement of resource extraction projects without the free, prior, and informed consent of Indigenous inhabitants. In northern Ontario, the Canadian government has granted mining rights on the Breathing Lands, a vast wetland ecosystem that serves as a massive carbon sink and biodiversity hub. This area, part of the Ring of Fire mineral deposit, holds enormous stores of nickel and other metals, yet extraction proceeds despite strong resistance from the Nishnawbe Aski Nation, which represents 49 communities. Kohen Mattinas, representing the nation at EMRIP, emphasized that the right to consent must be respected, noting that new Canadian legislation allows projects to be advanced through an accelerated process. In response, Mattinas co-founded Okiniwak, a youth organization that organizes rallies and engages in international advocacy to protect their land. This specific conflict illustrates a broader global trend where the demand for minerals for the energy transition drives further encroachment on Indigenous territories, often bypassing established human rights standards.

## The Burden on the Unrecognized

The stakes are particularly high for Indigenous groups in nations that do not legally recognize their status, leaving them with few domestic avenues for redress. Nichodimas Cooper, an Indigenous Nama from Botswana, explained that the lack of national recognition makes it difficult to effect change locally, as his people face forced assimilation and displacement. For communities like the Nama, the UN platform offers a rare opportunity to pressure states to respect inalienable rights. Similarly, Viro Xulue, a Kanak advisor from New Caledonia, linked the struggle against climate change directly to the ongoing process of decolonization. He stated that respecting self-determination is essential for protecting resources and securing a future for the next generation, noting that 170 years of colonization have left a persistent inequity for Kanak people. Even with legal victories, such as an International Court of Justice advisory opinion holding countries accountable for climate damage, the injustice remains unresolved on the ground. These examples demonstrate how historical colonization and modern climate policy intersect to maintain systemic vulnerability.

## Emerging Threats and Future Standards

Beyond physical land disputes, the delegates are also confronting emerging digital threats that could further erode Indigenous sovereignty. The session includes discussions on artificial intelligence and the push for data sovereignty, ensuring that technologies are not developed or deployed without free, prior, and informed consent. Dhamai expressed hope that EMRIP would provide guidance on these mounting issues, alongside climate finance and the global energy transition, so that Indigenous perspectives shape international standards rather than merely responding to them. As the 20th anniversary of the UN Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples approaches, the central question facing these delegates remains whether governments are prepared to uphold the rights they have already recognized. The gathering in Geneva makes visible the reality that without a fundamental shift in how extraction and governance operate, the overlapping crises of climate and conflict could continue to compound, leaving Indigenous peoples to absorb the initial and most severe damages of a destabilizing world.
